wildos-base (0.1.9) trixie; urgency=medium

  * The guest session from Settings > Privacy and security > Advanced
    protection: a Guest account on the sign-in screen with no password,
    a fresh empty Home in memory each time, and everything the guest
    left on the PC wiped when they sign out. The guest can't open other
    accounts' files, ask for an administrator's password, use Windows
    mode, share folders or install anything for the whole PC. Turning it
    off removes the account.

 -- Wild OS Developers <dev@wildos.io>  Sat, 14 Nov 2026 14:56:10 +0000

wildos-base (0.1.8) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Wild OS calls this release 0.1 Beta 2, and its support and problem
    report links go to the right place on GitHub.

 -- Wild OS Developers <dev@wildos.io>  Sat, 14 Nov 2026 14:53:57 +0000

wildos-base (0.1.7) trixie; urgency=medium

  * At Standard protection the kernel no longer forces Meltdown protection
    on processors that do not have the flaw, which made opening, copying
    and saving many files slower. Processors with the flaw still get it,
    and Extra processor protection, Strict and Maximum still force it.

 -- Wild OS Developers <dev@wildos.io>  Sat, 14 Nov 2026 14:17:55 +0000

wildos-base (0.1.6) trixie; urgency=medium

  * On a PC that also starts Windows (the installer wrote its start-up
    menu entry), the hardware clock is kept in local time as Windows keeps
    it, so the clock no longer starts hours off after using Windows and
    Windows' clock is no longer put off by Wild OS. Done once, at the next
    start-up.

 -- Wild OS Developers <dev@wildos.io>  Sat, 14 Nov 2026 08:45:00 +0000

wildos-base (0.1.5) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Linux 7.2 and Mesa 26.1: newer kernel and graphics, for speed, newer
    hardware and games. The previous kernel stays in the start-up menu.
  * NVIDIA's driver gets the version that builds for Linux 7.2.
  * "Wild OS (previous version)" in the start-up menu starts the last
    kernel that reached the desktop.

 -- Wild OS Developers <dev@wildos.io>  Fri, 13 Nov 2026 22:47:33 +0000

wildos-base (0.1.4) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Snappier under load: the kernel puts what you are using ahead of
    background work sooner.
  * When memory runs out, the app using the most is closed straight away
    instead of the whole computer freezing.
  * Encrypted SSDs stay fast: TRIM now reaches the drive, and disk
    encryption works without an extra queue.
  * The start-up image unpacks faster.

 -- Wild OS Developers <dev@wildos.io>  Fri, 13 Nov 2026 17:15:00 +0000

wildos-base (0.1.3) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Faster on most processors: every processor thread runs where the
    processor is safe for it, and protections for flaws the processor
    doesn't have are no longer forced on. Extra processor protection in
    Settings, and Strict and Maximum, keep the full set. Takes effect
    after a restart.

 -- Wild OS Developers <dev@wildos.io>  Fri, 13 Nov 2026 16:30:00 +0000

